00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:54.530
So here is the tone for
machine learning at Google,

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:59.160
is set by our former CEO and
current chairman Eric Schmidt.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.250
The way he describe
was interesting,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.590
think of the earlier
generation of programming

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.140
as telling machines what
they should do meticulously.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.440
And now we're
doing a transition,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.540
and we're in the early
part of the transition.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.930
From that imperative, here's
what the computer does,

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.600
I'll feed you data, I'll
get you started with models,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.310
you figure out what
the user wants,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.900
you figured out if
there is a fraud,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.000
you figured out what
the image means.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.100
And that is the kind of
interesting transition

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.404
that we're doing.

00:02:26.404 --> 00:02:28.070
We've been going
through this transition

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.520
internally for over a decade.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.090
And what this stock
tries to capture

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.740
is the confluence of what
the cloud products do,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.460
and the learnings from those
projects over the last 12 years

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.480
or so that combined from a
coherent machine learning

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.100
lifecycle.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.570
As in case of
software development

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.160
it's not just
about writing code,

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.590
here it's not just about
finding the right algorithm

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.660
and creating a model.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.900
It is all about the entire
lifecycle end to end.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.590
And just as there is this SDLC
software development lifecycle

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.260
that walks you through
requirements all the way to how

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.990
to make sure that you have
the right system deployed.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.760
Similarly in machine
learning, we're

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.000
going to take a quick look
at the entire lifecycle

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.650
and that's the main
topic of this talk.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.470
So here is the problem that
we're going to start with.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.920
I want to get an operational ML
model for some business problem

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.580
that I have, and it could be
in any of multiple domains.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.010
But before we start
on that, let's

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.690
have a quick show
of hands so that I

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.560
have a sense of where people
are at and what they have done.

