00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:35.240
All right.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:41.180
So with that let's
get to the lifecycle.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.920
The first step is to find
the business problem.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.304
Then a bunch of things
related to data,

00:04:46.304 --> 00:04:47.720
in fact machine
learning is really

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.360
not a statistical
machine learning

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.260
problem it is fundamentally
a big data problem.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.310
And tack onto that some advanced
machine learning algorithms

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.990
and engines and you've got
a good sort of progress

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.160
that you can make.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.900
You need to collect data
first, from various sources

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.820
logs, systems of record,
the transactional data.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.620
It might be some
external purchased data

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.090
like maps related, zip code,
demographic information,

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.210
whatever.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.960
You would bring that together
in a data preparation step.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.880
Make sure that it's
all brought together,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.590
so that you can
use it from machine

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:25.550
learning in a coherent single
sort of feature set form.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.070
And then you would
analyze that often

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.750
to do feature engineering.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.840
To figure out which
of the myriad columns

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.900
or which of the myriad features
are relevant for building

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.640
the right kind of model.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.490
And then you would honestly
step into the modeling part,

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.310
train a model,
evaluate the model

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.620
to make sure it meets
your requirements

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.750
or meets your error bar.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.570
And then optimize it to get
the last ounce of performance

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.400
that you can get
out of the model.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.960
And then the fun part starts.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.940
In fact when you're done
with model think of it

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:59.667
that you're done with maybe 60%,
70%, you've got at least 30%,

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.510
40% remaining.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.880
But you would take the
model and deploy that.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.900
Maybe deploy multiple models,
experiment with them and it

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.980
could be composite sense.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.360
Collect the data.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.290
And then manage the
lifecycle of the models,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.180
including rolling back.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:21.710
And we've got a set of products
that target different things.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.820
BigQuery, Dataflow, and
Dataproc are the products

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:26.730
that deal with big data.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:31.295
BigQuery does about SQL on up
to terabytes of data in a snap.

00:06:31.295 --> 00:06:33.670
Maybe by bringing together
thousands or Tens of thousands

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.920
of machines to run in query.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.030
Dataflow is hard on
streaming and batch data.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:45.310
And Ddataproc is our hosted
version of Spark and Hadoop.

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:48.580
And then the product
that went GA today,

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.040
machine learning engine,
cloud machine learning engine.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.680
And that product deals with
models, how to train them,

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.080
evaluate, optimize, and so on.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.620
And then Datalab provides Python
based experience, notebook

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.830
based experience,
for going through all

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:03.850
these different steps.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.670
And of course our studio,
the latest addition

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:11.020
that will allow you to do
similar things in AR as well.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.140
And then rounding out is
a set of services provided

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.960
by Google and by
partners like KPMG that

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.330
help you go through these.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:21.550
A slight segue here
I want to cover,

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.950
the machine learning
products are very briefly so

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.770
that we are clear on what we are
covering in the remaining talk

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:27.940
and what not.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.770
There is a set of
APIs that are mainly

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:31.620
meant for software developers.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.060
As in you don't have to
worry about creating model,

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.890
you don't even have to
worry about training data.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.640
We have data, we have models
all you do is make an API call,

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.610
or thousands of them
second, and we'll return

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:44.930
whatever information you need.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:49.220
For example speech to text, or
finding objects in an image,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.950
or the recent edition of
video intelligence where

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.460
it finds entities in a given
video at different points

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:56.690
in the timeline.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:58.455
And then that is
machine learning engine

00:07:58.455 --> 00:08:01.870
where we don't give you data,
we don't give you a model,

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.640
it's your data,
your model, we make

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.130
sure that we can run it at
scale for you, for training

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.200
and for prediction.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.320
So effectively it's
a way of taking

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:17.780
care of the systems aspect
of the machine learning part.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.490
So we're going to
talk exclusively

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:21.260
about machine
learning, I'm not going

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.780
to cover the app development
using API's, there

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:25.070
are separate talks for that.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:26.840
This is all about
machine learning

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.480
models and their lifecycle,
and for the life cycle

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.406
they have to be born so
we'll go through that.

00:08:31.406 --> 00:08:32.780
The cloud machine
learning engine

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:37.010
itself is based on Tensorflow,
an open source project that's

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.559
been getting a lot of attention
and we are gratified by that.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:42.620
A lot of research
interest, a lot of product

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.180
interest as well
from third parties.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.950
So you can think of it as
the machine learning engine

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:52.640
sitting on top of TensorFlow
and providing scalable managed

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.110
service.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:55.670
It also connects
with other services,

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:58.226
and we'll see examples
of BigQuery and Dataflow.

00:08:58.226 --> 00:09:02.870
And as of today we have
GPU's available in beta so

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:06.790
you can run your
training much faster.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:09.610
The life cycle support the rest
of the talk will covered that.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.580
And then it's completely
open, the principle

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.160
behind cloud machine
learning engine

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:17.410
is you can take your
model, is your model

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.200
you can take it out
anytime you want

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.410
or you can bring it
anytime you want.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.830
So for example there are
companies that do training

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.230
at scale on machine
learning engine,

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:29.050
but then they take that model
and they run it on premise

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.060
or wherever they are running
their production system.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.060
And that's fine too,
that's an available option.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.516
You can do the reverse
as well, bring in a model

00:09:36.516 --> 00:09:38.140
and you want to take
care of prediction

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.889
we'll do that as well.

00:09:40.889 --> 00:09:42.430
And so there's a
set of products that

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.850
support that we're going to
go through all the details.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:49.850
So let's get back
to the lifecycle,

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.820
for this lifecycle the
best way to think about it

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.160
is in an iterative fashion.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.430
Same life cycle of splitted sort
of into the data versus model

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:01.980
versus the deployed
system aspects.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:03.840
You can think of
it as a waterfall,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.600
just for simplicity
but in reality

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.030
it will never be a waterfall.

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:10.130
Whenever you're preparing
data and analyzing

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.926
you might discover that you
just don't have enough data.

00:10:12.926 --> 00:10:14.300
In fact we'll see
the census demo

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.569
where the data isn't
all that great,

00:10:16.569 --> 00:10:18.110
and that's sort of
the point that you

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:20.810
have to go back get more data.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:23.490
Then when you're doing
modeling it's the same.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.240
Usually you would start with
the simplest algorithm possible,

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.090
maybe in a linear regression
for structured data.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.670
There are a few rules of thumb
you would follow and just use

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:32.390
those.

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.790
But then when you find that
your model quality isn't

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:36.840
exactly what you want,
you would go back

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.170
make changes and train again.

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:41.960
After you're done with that to
get additional performance out

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:45.380
of it you might do
hyperparameter optimization.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.560
Like number of learns in a
neural network, learning rate,

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.760
and those kinds of parameters.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.120
You might queue them
and get a better model.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.644
Even in the deployment
management sense,

00:10:56.644 --> 00:10:59.060
you'd be running experiments
and if your new model doesn't

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:01.370
perform in production,
no matter how well it

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:05.390
does in your notebook or in
your data science platform,

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.310
you'll pull back and
revert to the old one,

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:11.070
or you might turn multiple
ones and pick the right one.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.030
And not only that, you might
go back based on an experiment

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.940
and say, hey, user
behavior has changed.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.340
Maybe a new app
has launched, users

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:22.910
don't do what they used to
do 3 months ago or a week ago

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:23.910
in fact.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.060
And I'm going to
collect more data

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.910
and figure out how to
improve my predictions.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:34.020
For the demos we're going to
focus on the blue box here,

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:39.690
that covers from analysis
to very basic deployment.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:43.330
And we will not go into the
problem definition and so on.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:46.560
But because of that, I just
give it one slide, it's new.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.540
This is actually the
most important part

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.577
of the lifecycle.

00:11:49.577 --> 00:11:51.660
As they say "If you don't
know where you're going,

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:52.935
any road will take you there."

00:11:52.935 --> 00:11:54.810
So this is the one about
knowing where you're

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.920
going, if you have crisp
business objectives,

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.792
and I've listed three
at the top that happened

00:12:00.792 --> 00:12:03.000
to be the ones that we're
going to go to in the demo,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.550
that very sort of demo
centric quick ones.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.150
And then I've
listed another five

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:10.440
that are actual industry
problems that have

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:12.670
been stalled using CloudML.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.370
I'll only briefly
allude to those.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.560
