00:41:09.675 --> 00:41:11.270
Today, I want to
briefly show you

00:41:11.270 --> 00:41:14.120
some of the work that we've
been doing with both TensorFlow

00:41:14.120 --> 00:41:17.900
and Cloud ML and R. So we'll
talk about the TensorFlow R

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:18.540
package.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:20.540
We'll talk about a new
package, called Cloud ML.

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:22.430
I'll give you a
quick demo that's

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:24.590
doing some prediction
on the census income,

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:26.150
same census income
data set that we

00:41:26.150 --> 00:41:31.120
saw before, and talk about what
we're working on next as well.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.290
So first, the
TensorFlow package.

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:36.650
This is an R package that
actually gives access

00:41:36.650 --> 00:41:38.840
to the entire TensorFlow API.

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:41.095
The API is mostly
defined in Python.

00:41:41.095 --> 00:41:42.470
This R package
actually gives you

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:44.900
access to all the modules,
functions, and classes

00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:47.490
that are in the API,
and as a result,

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:51.590
R code looks very similar to
the equivalent Python code.

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:54.530
In this case, you can see the
Hello World is line for line,

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:56.950
almost character for
character, the same.

00:41:56.950 --> 00:41:59.780
The TensorFlow R package
works with all versions

00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:01.754
of TensorFlow, so
it sort of binds

00:42:01.754 --> 00:42:03.920
to whatever version of
TensorFlow is on your machine

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:05.950
and uses that.

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:09.770
A couple of quick examples,
this is the MNIST softmax model

00:42:09.770 --> 00:42:12.260
that you see in the
TensorFlow tutorials.

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:13.850
You can see I'm
making some tensors.

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:15.580
I'm creating a session.

00:42:15.580 --> 00:42:18.420
I am processing some training
data and making predictions.

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:22.370
The code looks very much like
the equivalent Python code.

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:25.520
Here's a slightly more
sophisticated example

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:31.670
that uses flags, uses some name
scopes, does some summaries,

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:35.500
builds functions, builds
layers using functions.

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:39.950
All again, if you've seen the
tutorials to do this in Python,

00:42:39.950 --> 00:42:43.610
works very similar in R. So
that's the TensorFlow package,

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:45.470
and that's kind of
the basis of the work

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:49.635
that we've done with Cloud ML.

00:42:49.635 --> 00:42:52.760
So the Cloud ML package,
what is that about?

00:42:52.760 --> 00:42:54.200
Our goal with the
Cloud ML package

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:56.780
is to interact with
the Cloud ML service,

00:42:56.780 --> 00:42:59.152
typically use the Google
Cloud SDK command line tools.

00:42:59.152 --> 00:43:01.610
What we wanted to do is create
very similar to what we just

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:04.640
saw, a local
development workflow

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:07.190
that looks very much like a
standard R local development

00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:09.980
workflow, and that with
one gesture, one function

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:12.980
call or one button, you can
take that local work you did

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:15.800
and then train it on Cloud ML.

00:43:15.800 --> 00:43:19.070
So over on that source
code, we sort of see

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:20.490
what that looks like.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:21.880
You work locally
in a file called

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:24.940
train.r that trains
whatever model it

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:27.730
is that you're working on.

00:43:27.730 --> 00:43:29.309
You then, when
you're ready to go,

00:43:29.309 --> 00:43:31.100
when you think you've
got the model working

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:34.460
the way you want it to, you can
do an emulated local run of it.

00:43:34.460 --> 00:43:35.609
You say train local.

00:43:35.609 --> 00:43:37.400
It'll do the same thing
that would normally

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.191
happen on Cloud ML,
but it'll do it locally

00:43:39.191 --> 00:43:42.360
to make sure all the parts
are connected properly.

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:44.540
And then, when you're
ready to work on Cloud ML,

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:45.860
you call train cloud ML.

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:48.950
That's an asynchronous call that
will basically create the job

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:50.790
and give you a handle to it.

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:53.620
And then, when the job is
done, you can collect it.

00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:56.755
And as Dinesh said, now you
have that model, you can do it.

00:43:56.755 --> 00:43:58.130
You can put it on
a Raspberry Pi.

00:43:58.130 --> 00:43:59.840
You can use it locally.

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:02.720
You can put it back up into
Cloud ML for prediction.

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:03.837
It's your model.

00:44:03.837 --> 00:44:05.420
And then, we'll also
be adding support

00:44:05.420 --> 00:44:10.610
for doing a hyper parameter
tuning in Cloud ML as well.

00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:14.289
So let's actually look
at how that works.

00:44:14.289 --> 00:44:15.830
The model that I'm
showing here, this

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:18.830
is basically a model
to predict income

00:44:18.830 --> 00:44:20.870
from a number of features.

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:25.130
It's using the TFLearn APIs,
defining a bunch of columns,

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:26.960
defining a bunch of layers.

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:28.645
It's creating a
TFLearn estimator.

00:44:28.645 --> 00:44:30.770
I'm not going to go into
what all these things are,

00:44:30.770 --> 00:44:33.440
but I'm just showing you
sort of the flexibility

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:37.160
that you have in terms
of defining models.

00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:40.590
We're going to create some input
functions, all the things you

00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:43.520
would normally do to build
a TFLearn stop model.

00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:45.530
And then you've got this
file, train.r, which

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:47.480
is where you train the model.

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:52.067
You can build the estimator,
call learn, run, or run,

00:44:52.067 --> 00:44:52.650
and it trains.

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:54.980
So I'll just source that,
and it'll start training.

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:57.380
That's, again, a
local training you

00:44:57.380 --> 00:44:59.409
want to work on a sample
set of local data,

00:44:59.409 --> 00:45:00.950
make sure the model
works the way you

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:03.950
want it to before you
submit it to the cloud.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:05.540
And we want the
same exact source

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:07.850
code to work locally
and on the cloud.

00:45:07.850 --> 00:45:10.844
And to do that, we have this
config file, which basically

00:45:10.844 --> 00:45:13.010
says, well, what varies
between local and the cloud,

00:45:13.010 --> 00:45:16.670
while the input data, the
training and evaluation data

00:45:16.670 --> 00:45:19.370
differs, where you
rate the model differs,

00:45:19.370 --> 00:45:21.870
and training parameters
themselves might differ.

00:45:21.870 --> 00:45:24.012
So we've got the default
local configuration,

00:45:24.012 --> 00:45:25.970
and then, we've got the
Cloud ML configuration.

00:45:25.970 --> 00:45:28.386
You can see there, I'm going
to point to some Google Cloud

00:45:28.386 --> 00:45:31.190
Storage buckets that might
have much larger data sets.

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:33.650
I'm going to actually put the
job in a Google Cloud Storage

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:36.080
bucket, where I can publish
it for prediction later,

00:45:36.080 --> 00:45:38.540
or download it, and
I may change some

00:45:38.540 --> 00:45:40.275
of the training parameters.

00:45:40.275 --> 00:45:43.490
All right, so it's go back here.

00:45:43.490 --> 00:45:47.060
The local training job
completed, and returned an R

00:45:47.060 --> 00:45:48.625
list with a bunch of metrics.

00:45:48.625 --> 00:45:50.750
So let's see what it would
look like to actually do

00:45:50.750 --> 00:45:51.740
that in the cloud.

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:56.310
Same code, I'll
say train cloudml.

00:45:56.310 --> 00:45:59.800
And it's going to submit
the job to Cloud ML.

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:01.550
And once that's
submitted, it'll print out

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:03.590
a bunch of information
saying, you know,

00:46:03.590 --> 00:46:06.680
here's the URL in
the cloud console

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:07.920
where you can go look at it.

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:09.020
You can see here,
it says OK, if you

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:11.150
want to view the job
online, here's where it is.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:13.370
Here's where the logs are.

00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:14.975
I can check the
status of the job.

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:21.320
And then, ultimately, what I
want to do, is collect the job.

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:24.030
And when I collect the job, it's
actually going to download it.

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:25.730
Right now, the job
is still working,

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:27.063
so it's not going to collect it.

00:46:28.964 --> 00:46:31.380
The model will be downloaded
into a local directory, where

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:33.510
I can then do whatever
I want with it.

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:34.980
And in this case,
I'm going to show

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:36.980
some of the things you
might do with that model,

00:46:36.980 --> 00:46:39.600
because in the evaluation
phase, you often

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:43.845
want to take the predictions
and either run various automated

00:46:43.845 --> 00:46:45.970
diagnostics on them, to
see if they're good enough.

00:46:45.970 --> 00:46:49.290
But you also may want to
do some data visualizations

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:50.790
and work with them
interactively.

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:55.140
So here I'm going to
show different ways

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:58.830
to then create predictions out
of the model you've downloaded.

00:46:58.830 --> 00:47:04.200
One of them I have here,
I'm going to basically plot

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:08.010
what the predictions were for
occupation based on income

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:10.920
brackets so I can get an
intuitive feel for what kind

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:13.440
of predictions are being made.

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:15.900
Another thing I can do, since
I've got the model local,

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.491
is I can build an
interactive tool

00:47:18.491 --> 00:47:19.740
for exploring the predictions.

00:47:19.740 --> 00:47:22.110
This is a shiny
dashboard, that's

00:47:22.110 --> 00:47:24.210
going to basically
take some test data,

00:47:24.210 --> 00:47:29.220
and is going to then do local
predictions based on the model

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:32.527
that I've downloaded, and let me
explore the kind of predictions

00:47:32.527 --> 00:47:33.360
that are being made.

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:36.550
So this is showing
the various features

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:41.070
for the different levels
of the features, what

00:47:41.070 --> 00:47:42.360
was the income prediction.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:44.130
I can change the
prediction cutoff

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:46.890
and see how that changes
both the overall accuracy

00:47:46.890 --> 00:47:48.390
of the model, and
then the confusion

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:50.070
matrix you see in the top right.

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:53.970
So really, the idea here is
that you can work locally in R,

00:47:53.970 --> 00:47:57.230
you can do the difficult
heavy lifting of training,

00:47:57.230 --> 00:47:59.430
an expensive to train
model, or a model

00:47:59.430 --> 00:48:01.680
you want to do hyper-parameter
tuning on in the cloud,

00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:03.221
and then you can
bring it back local,

00:48:03.221 --> 00:48:05.670
and do all the things you
are accustomed to doing,

00:48:05.670 --> 00:48:09.932
when you're doing
modeling with R.

00:48:09.932 --> 00:48:12.442
So what are we working on next?

00:48:12.442 --> 00:48:14.650
The TensorFlow packets I've
shown, that it's actually

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:17.020
been in development
for about six months,

00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:20.290
and that's going to be on
CRAN by the end of this month.

00:48:20.290 --> 00:48:22.860
The Cloud ML package
I showed today,

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:24.110
we're working on it in GitHub.

00:48:24.110 --> 00:48:27.340
There will be a beta available
by the end of the month.

00:48:27.340 --> 00:48:31.230
And then, the TensorFlow
API, as it currently stands,

00:48:31.230 --> 00:48:35.120
is really a very close
mapping to the Python API.

00:48:35.120 --> 00:48:38.090
And what we really want to
do, for the higher level

00:48:38.090 --> 00:48:42.250
TensorFlow APIs that are
coming from TFLearn and Keras

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:46.410
in TensorFlow 1.1, we want to
create fluent, pure functional

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:50.380
R APIs that make it even
easier to use TensorFlow

00:48:50.380 --> 00:48:55.900
from within R. So with that,
Hongfei from KPMG will talk.

